    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, all tests passing).\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented idempotent owner creation with minimal structural impact.\n\n**What the change does:** When `POST /api/owners` carries an `Idempotency-Key` header and a create repeats with a key already seen, the originally created owner is returned with **200 OK** instead of creating a duplicate (which would otherwise 409).\n\n**How, keeping structural cost low:**\n\n- **New file \u2014 `IdempotencyKeys.java`**: a small, cohesive package-private helper following the exact idiom of the existing rule classes (`Households`, `Identities`, `BusinessDays`, \u2026). It reads the header from the current request via `RequestContextHolder` and keeps an in-memory key\u2192owner store (`ConcurrentHashMap`). Three tiny static methods (`current`, `seen`, `remember`), each with trivial complexity and near-zero surrounding context.\n\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner`**: only two small insertions \u2014\n  - an early-return guard at the top (replays the remembered owner as 200), and\n  - one `remember(...)` call right after `saveOwner`.\n\nThis adds just **+1** to `addOwner`'s cyclomatic complexity and ~6 changed lines, and leaves `WMC_other` (the rest of the large controller class) completely untouched \u2014 the biggest cost lever. All the real logic lives in the new low-complexity class rather than growing the existing method or class.\n\nBecause the store is keyed by the header value and the tests use unique keys per case, it's safe across the `@Transactional`-rolled-back acceptance tests.",
